  "query_preview": "..\n\nAnother pleasant day today as surface high pressure remains centered \nover the central Great Lakes with most areas seeing highs around 80. \nSkies clear again overnight with the loss of diurnal cu allowing \nlows again to fall towards the mid-upper 50s. Surface ridge axis \nshifts over southern Ontario Saturday setting up light southwesterly \nlow level return flow pushing temps back around late July averages \nin the mid 80s. This shift also marks the expansion of broad central \nCONUS mid-level ridging into the far western Great Lakes setting the \nstage for a more active close of the weekend locally as a series of \nridge riding shortwaves drop into the region. \n\nFirst of these waves sweeps across lower MI Saturday evening with no \nfanfare given the resident dry airmass. Its passage however does \nbegin to fold in a piece of the richer theta-e airmass over the \nupper Midwest with gradual advection top-down in the column over the \ncourse of Saturday night. Lower level moisture looks to become \nestablished by early Sunday morning, coinciding with the arrival of \nthe second wave, supporting scattered shower chances, particularly \nover the Saginaw Valley who reside under the left exit region of the \nupper Midwestern jet streak. A few embedded thunderstorms not out of \nthe question as several J/kg of elevated instability accompany this \nmoisture advection. This activity wanes by midday Sunday as the wave \nreaches Lake Erie/northern OH leading more importantly to a window \nfor afternoon destabilization ahead of the next wave reaching the \narea in the evening. While SE MI is only under the edge of SPC's Day \n3 marginal currently, this shortwave shouldn't be discounted with \ncurrent mid-range model solutions all advertising an arrival in the \n~21-00Z timeframe (NAM is a touch later towards 03Z). Favorable \ntiming within the diurnal window would support MLCAPE values between \n1000-1300 J/kg with bulk shear around 35-40kts. The main potential \nlimiting factor, seen best in the 12Z RRFS, is a more robust morning \nconvective scenario resulting in stronger trailing \nsubsidence/capping that any evening convection would need to \novercome first. \n\nSome lingering showers/storms are possible to start Monday morning \nthough the main convective chances come late Monday as the next wave \nreaches the western Great Lakes. Timing window is more muddled as \nmodel solutions range from late Monday afternoon (Canadian/NAM) to \nMonday night (GFS/ECMWF). That said, this system brings the \nstrongest kinematics of any in the wave train as a mid-level trough \ndigs into the upper Great Lakes. As such, even in the overnight \narrival scenarios, organized severe convection would be in play (all \nof SE MI is under a SPC Day 4 15% risk outlook).",
